NODE_ENV=development

# Hosts spider may visit (subdomains included). Everything else is refused before a browser opens it.
# Dev default: localhost,127.0.0.1,linkedin.com,deviceandbrowserinfo.com,pixelscan.net (LinkedIn on the operator's
# attestation of LinkedIn's written permission, see .plan/linkedin-account-linking.md; the last two are the bot
# detection pages the browser test visits, see .plan/spider-browser-test.md). Production has no default and must list
# its targets.
# SPIDER_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1,linkedin.com,deviceandbrowserinfo.com,pixelscan.net

# Hosts (a subset of SPIDER_ALLOWED_HOSTS) whose robots.txt is not checked. Empty by default; dev default:
# linkedin.com, because LinkedIn's robots.txt disallows every unlisted agent. Rate limits still apply.
# SPIDER_ROBOTS_EXEMPT_HOSTS=linkedin.com

# AMQP URL of the RabbitMQ broker (vhost in the path). The default matches the docker compose broker.
# RABBITMQ_URL=amqp://automatedlinkedin:automatedlinkedin@localhost:5672/automatedlinkedin

# Google Cloud Storage bucket for sessions/ (read) and results/ (write). Worker only.
# Credentials come from Application Default Credentials: for a local run use an account that can write to the
# bucket (`gcloud auth application-default login`), on GKE use Workload Identity.
# SPIDER_GCS_BUCKET=automate-linkedin-511215-spider

# Politeness: a fixed gap between two actions on the same host, and a cap on records per task.
# SPIDER_MIN_DELAY_MS=2000
# SPIDER_MAX_RECORDS=200
# SPIDER_ACTION_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# Agent token matched against robots.txt rules.
# SPIDER_ROBOTS_USER_AGENT=automatedlinkedin-spider

# One task per account at a time. A task holds its account for at most the lock ttl (longer than the longest
# task); a task that finds the account busy waits the busy delay and is tried again.
# SPIDER_ACCOUNT_LOCK_TTL_MS=1800000
# SPIDER_ACCOUNT_BUSY_DELAY_MS=10000

# Account validation. Brain sends the credentials sealed with LINKEDIN_CRED_ENC_KEY, which must be the same key brain
# uses (dev default: the same throwaway key as brain). The worker signs in once and never repeats a failed login.
# LINKEDIN_CRED_ENC_KEY=
# full: try the saved browser token, then the password (and a two-step code). session: only the token.
# SPIDER_VALIDATION_MODE=full
# Only a test site differs from this. Its host must be in SPIDER_ALLOWED_HOSTS.
# SPIDER_LINKEDIN_BASE_URL=https://www.linkedin.com

# Record each task run (account logins, browser detection) for the Sessions page (.plan/spider-session-recording.md): the steps, the requests, the
# console and a video, in the bucket under recordings/. off (production default), failures (keep a recording only
# when the run went wrong) or always (dev default). A recording shows what the browser showed, so keep it off unless
# you need it. Video needs Playwright's ffmpeg: `pnpm --filter @automatedLinkedIn/spider exec playwright-core install ffmpeg`.
# SPIDER_RECORDING=always
# SPIDER_RECORD_VIDEO=true

# How the browser runs. true (default) is headless Firefox. virtual runs a real browser on an Xvfb display, which
# Camoufox advises over plain headless (.plan/spider-virtual-display.md). Linux only, and Xvfb must be installed
# (`sudo apt-get install xvfb`); the worker image has it and sets virtual, and the staging overlay sets true. Not
# available on macOS.
# SPIDER_HEADLESS=true

# The worker answers GET /health on this port for the Service status page: connected to the queue, consuming, and able
# to read the bucket. Brain reads it; it is not a Kubernetes probe.
# SPIDER_WORKER_HEALTH_PORT=3003
